  "worked_solution": "To get rid of the decimals, we multiply the second equation by $10,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}4x+3y=15 &  \\\\ 0.6x-0.8y=1 & |\\,\\times 10\\end{aligned}\\,\u27f9\\,{\\begin{aligned}4x+3y=15 \\\\ 6x-8y=10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that if we multiply the first equation by $3$ and the second equation by $-2,$ we will get a $12x$ term in the first equation that can cancel with the $-12x$ term in the second equation:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}4x+3y=15 & |\\,\\times 3 \\\\ 6x-8y=10 & |\\,\\times -2\\end{aligned}\\,\u27f9\\,{\\begin{aligned}12x+9y=45 \\\\ -12x+16y=-20\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the two equations eliminates the $x$-variable and allows us to solve for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\cancel{12x}+9y & =45 \\\\ -\\cancel{12x}+16y & =-20 \\\\ (9y+16y) & =(45-20) \\\\ 25y & =25 \\\\ y & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can substitute $y=1$ into the equation $4x+3y=15$ to solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4x+3y & =15 \\\\ 4x+3 & =15 \\\\ 4x & =15-3 \\\\ 4x & =12 \\\\ x & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the solution to the system of equations is $x=3$ and $y=1.$ So, \n\n$$\nx+y=3+1=4.\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
